<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tajikistan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Tajikistan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tajikistan, officially the Republic of Tajikistan, is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Parts of its territory belonged to the Sogdian and Bactrian civilisations and were ruled in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a>, before conquest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Its borders were drawn while it was part of Uzbekistan as the Tajik ASSR, and it became a Soviet republic on 5 December 1929. Tajikistan declared independence on 9 September 1991. A civil war followed from May 1992 to June 1997, with estimated deaths above 100,000.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Takht-i Sangin in <b>Tajikistan</b> held a large temple to the Oxus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Mirziyoyev relations with Kyrgyzstan, <b>Tajikistan</b> and Afghanistan improved drastically, and ties with China, Turkey, Russia, India, South Korea, Japan, the UAE and the West have grown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Middle Persian is the linguistic ancestor of New Persian, the official language of Iran, of Afghanistan as Dari and of <b>Tajikistan</b> as Tajik.</blockquote><a href="/language/middle-persian">Middle Persian · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Tajikistan</b>, Persian has been written in the Cyrillic alphabet since 1940.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>In the highlands of <b>Tajikistan</b>, kurgan cemeteries of the Vaksh and Bishkent types mixed late BMAC and Tazabagyab-Andronovo traditions, and at Sappali Tepe metal objects of 1700–1500 BCE derive from the Tazabagyab-Andronovo culture.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sogdians were an Iranian people whose homeland, Sogdiana, lay between the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, the Oxus and Jaxartes of the Greeks, in present-day Uzbekistan and <b>Tajikistan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
